{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And Elohim blessed the seventh day.</b> For on it the world was filled with all good and blessing and satiation — like 'אשר ברכך HaShem your Elohim you shall give to him' — which is why we say in prayer, and His blessing is more than all the [other] days." "<b>And He sanctified it.</b> Rashi's comment: the manna did not fall on it. And if you ask: the festivals too are sanctified with regard to manna, as it is written, 'and on the seventh day, a sabbath, there shall not be in it' — and our Rabbis taught: the verse comes only to include Yom Kippur and festivals. One may answer: festivals do not leave the category of Sabbath, for they too are called Sabbath in many places, and from the verse <b>and He sanctified it</b> we learn [the laws of] Sabbaths and festivals. Another interpretation: <b>and He sanctified it</b> — from the doing of work, as it is written, <b>for in it He rested</b>, that He did no work in it. And so it says, 'and on the seventh day, a sabbath of solemn rest, a holy convocation; all work shall you not do.'" "<b>For in it He rested.</b> This is not the language of resting, for concerning the Holy One, blessed be He, it is written, 'He neither faints nor is weary,' and He created His world without effort — rather it is the language of cessation, like 'and the manna ceased' (וישבת המן), 'and the three men ceased' (ושבתו), and 'day and night shall not cease' (לא ישבתו). The meaning of <b>for in it</b> — He ceased from doing work." "<b>From all His work which He created</b> etc. — that from that time onward their practice would be <b>to make</b> the like of them, such as living creatures, trees, grasses — they sow and produce the like of themselves. Another interpretation: <b>to make</b> — all the days of the world, in the order of the days of Creation, that the heavens and the earth would follow their practice and not change the order. From here is a refutation of the heretics who say that the Holy One, blessed be He, does work on the Sabbath when He brings down rain and grows plants — and this is vanity, for all this was fixed from the six days of Creation: they were commanded to follow their practice, and this is not 'work.' And a proof for the matter is that indeed it is so, for since the manna was not created to fall during the six days of Creation, the Holy One, blessed be He, did not wish to bring it down on the Sabbath — given that the time of its initial creation was not seen until the Exodus from Egypt. Another interpretation: <b>which He created to make</b> — and did not make: these are the harmful spirits, whose souls the Holy One, blessed be He, created, but as it were He did not have time to create their bodies before the day was sanctified upon Him, and they were created at twilight (בין השמשות), and they have no body — they see and are not seen. Another interpretation: <b>to make</b> — all the making mentioned in the work of Creation is a matter of establishing and completing; thus the meaning is: from all the works which He created to establish the world through them. The reason 'and it was evening and it was morning, the seventh day' was not written is because there was no evening in it, but all of it was light." "<b>[To make — for in it He rested from making, from all.]</b>"], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:02:31.125486400Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}